    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d402">

                <pb facs="0583" n="483" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg483"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A14/695</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Persia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornibrook</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 45</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-08-28">August 28,
                        1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to previous correspondence
                    regarding the activities of the Persian Minister in Washington in connection
                    with the ratification by the United States of the Arms Traffic Convention signed
                    at Geneva in 1925, there is enclosed a copy of a note which the Minister
                    addressed to the Secretary of State on August 11, 1934.<anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d402-f1"/> The general tone of this communication is so obviously
                    improper and it contains so many misleading statements and misstatements that
                    the Secretary considered <pb facs="0584" n="484" xml:id="pg484"/> it necessary
                    to send the Minister a reply under date of August 24, of which a copy is
                        enclosed.<anchor n="56" xml:id="d402-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>You may on a suitable occasion, if you consider that any useful purpose would be
                    served, refer casually to the substance of the Secretary’s present
                    communication, in conversation with competent Persian officials.</p>
                <p>There are also transmitted, for the information of the Legation, a copy of a
                    letter which the Minister addressed to me on August 21,<anchor n="57"
                        xml:id="d402-f3"/> as well as a copy of a note which he sent to the
                    Secretary on August 25<anchor n="58" xml:id="d402-f4"/> in reply to the
                    Secretary’s note of August 24.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
